mode: "100644"
text: "# Audience\n\nThe goal is not a demographic table. It's to know **who the content is for**, what they\nalready believe, what they want, and where they pay attention — sharply enough that a skill\ncan write something that stops *them* specifically.\n\n## Capture one or two segments, not \"everyone\"\n\nSocial rewards specificity. A post written for everyone lands with no one. If the user\ninsists on a broad audience, push for the **one segment that moves the business most right\nnow**, and build for them. You can add segments later.\n\nFor each segment, capture:\n\n- **Who they are** — role/identity in plain terms (\"ops leads at 50–500-person companies,\"\n  \"new parents in the first year,\" \"indie game devs\"). Demographics only if they actually\n  change the content.\n- **What they care about** — their goals, the outcome they want, what keeps them up at night.\n- **What they already believe** — their current mental model, so content can meet them there\n  or challenge it deliberately.\n- **Objections / friction** — why they hesitate, what they're skeptical of. Great content\n  answers objections before they're voiced.\n- **Where they are** — which platforms, and in what mindset on each.\n- **Sophistication level** — beginner vs expert changes vocabulary, depth, and how much you\n  explain.\n\n## Buyer vs follower — especially in B2B\n\nThe person who *buys* and the person who *follows and shares on social* are often different.\nA CFO approves the purchase; the finance analyst follows the brand, shares the post, and\nchampions it internally. Capture both when they differ, and note which one a given content\npillar is really for. Writing only to the economic buyer makes for dry, unshareable social.\n\n## Jobs to be done (a sharper lens than personas)\n\nOften more useful than a persona: what \"job\" is someone hiring this brand's content to do?\n\n- To learn something that makes them better at their work.\n- To feel seen / less alone in a struggle.\n- To be entertained in a dead minute.\n- To look smart by sharing it.\n- To decide whether to buy.\n\nKnowing the job tells a skill what *shape* the content should take, not just the topic.\n\n## The transformation\n\nIn one line: where is this audience *before* the brand, and where are they *after*? This\nbefore→after is the spine of most high-performing content and the thing many profiles miss.\n\n## By business type\n\n- **B2B:** separate buyer and practitioner; lead with the practitioner for social reach.\n- **Ecommerce / DTC:** segment by use-case or moment (gifting vs self-purchase; the problem\n  the product solves) more than by demographic.\n- **Local / SMB:** geography and community are the segmentation; \"people within 5 miles who\n  care about X.\"\n- **Creator / personal brand:** the audience is whoever resonates with the person; capture the\n  specific kind of person who's \"their people.\"\n- **Nonprofit:** often *two* audiences — beneficiaries and donors/supporters — with very\n  different content needs. Note both.\n\n## Don't over-build\n\nTwo sharp segments beat eight vague ones. The test: could a writer who has never met this\naudience picture a real person and write to them? If yes, you have enough. If no, ask one\nmore question.\n\n## Keep it alive — the profile is a snapshot, the audience is a stream\n\nThis section captures today's best understanding; the loop keeps it true. Route ongoing\nsignals back here: `audience-research` for deeper personas, `interactive-content` (every poll\nis a micro-survey), `community-management` (what the human hears in comments and DMs), and\n`idea-generation-and-ideation` (clustered audience questions). When the signals contradict\nthe profile, the profile updates — not the other way around.\n"
